{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED at exact clean pushed head bf589838360a52e3aaac884611348f4eb84a0710. One defect, demonstrated rather than argued, and the fix is one line.\n\nWHAT IS RIGHT, and it is most of it. There is no rm, rm -f or rm -rf anywhere in the changed how-to — I checked by exit code, not by an empty grep. Removal uses unlink for files and rmdir for directories, and rmdir refuses a non-empty directory, so even a wrong target cannot be recursively destroyed. The cleanup validates the exact resolved path twice by string equality before touching anything, not merely its existence. And the comment credits the right thing: it says explicitly that find is not a safeguard and that the comparisons are, which is exactly the attribution the conditions asked for and the opposite of the usual mistake.\n\nThe pwd -P reasoning is correct and necessary, and I checked it rather than accepting it. git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir returns a path with symlinks already resolved, while $COPY may still contain one; on macOS a temporary directory under /tmp or /var is reached through exactly such a link, so comparing the two spellings would fail on every run. cd \"$COPY\" \u0026\u0026 pwd -P normalises the whole path, and it also disposes of a trailing slash and a relative input. A non-existent path makes cd fail, leaving COPY_DIR empty, so the comparison fails closed. The comparison is not weaker than it appears in any case I could construct.\n\nTHE DEFECT. The guards are enforced by the test harness, not by the page, and the page claims otherwise.\n\ninternal/docset/examples_test.go:110 writes the preamble \"set -euo pipefail\\ncd \\\"$SCRATCH\\\"\\n\" ahead of every extracted block and runs it under bash. So under make test the two test lines abort the script. No block in this page sets -e itself.\n\nA reader does not get that preamble. The page says \"Every command runs\", and a reader pastes the block into an ordinary interactive shell, where a failing test is a no-op and execution continues to the next line.\n\nI demonstrated both halves rather than reasoning about them. With a deliberately mismatched COPY_DIR, standing in for the substitution failure the guard exists to catch:\n\nUnder bash with the harness preamble, guard one fails, the script exits 1, and the target file survives.\n\nWithout set -e, guard one exits 1, guard two exits 1, GITSEQ_DIR is still computed from git's own answer, find runs, and the file is deleted. Both guards failed and the deletion happened anyway.\n\nSo the comment's sentence — \"a substitution returning anything else stops the block instead of deleting\" — is true under the gate and false in the reader's shell. That is the sentence a reader relies on, and it is the one place the page is not honest about its own safety. The guards are decorative exactly where they matter most: on a machine nobody gated.\n\nWHY I AM BLOCKING ON SOMETHING THIS SMALL. This lane has already produced two destructive-command findings, both from reader-facing blocks. The blast radius here is genuinely narrower than those — unlink and rmdir cannot flatten a tree, and GITSEQ_DIR derives from git's resolution rather than from raw substitution, so the realistic worst case is the correct directory of the wrong repository. But the page states a safety property it does not have, and a reader cannot check that claim without reading the test harness. Correcting a false safety claim is worth one round.\n\nWHAT WOULD CLEAR IT. Either add set -e to that block so the guards bind in the reader's shell as well as the gate's, or reword the comment so it does not claim the comparisons stop the block when they only stop the gate. I would take either, and I have no preference between them; the first is stronger, the second is honest. If planner judges the blast radius small enough that the sentence is acceptable as written, say so and I will re-review on that basis rather than insisting.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Documentation only, at layer 7's reader surface. No package boundary, contract, or layer claim is touched, and docs/reference/architecture.md needs no update.\n\nSECURITY. The removal path is the whole of the security surface here and it is analysed above: no rm, non-recursive primitives, exact resolved-path equality, fail-closed on a missing path. The one unresolved issue is the enforcement gap between gate and reader, which is the finding.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. The block is already close to minimal for what it must prove, and the comment earns its length by naming what is and is not a safeguard.\n\nGATES, run by me at the exact head in a clean detached checkout: internal/docset with -count=1, ok at 74.222s, uncached. Checkout clean.\n\nONE THING I DID NOT VERIFY. The conditions ask whether the stated limit about a forked sequence having no merge is honest framing. I could not locate that statement in the page at this head — my search for it returned nothing — so I am not judging it rather than guessing at which sentence is meant. Point me at it and I will answer in the re-review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8a0d7203e366524343118fe5a945ee4dc9e551aa","head":"bf589838360a52e3aaac884611348f4eb84a0710","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2dedc46936d981976b6c3088e5eba080f3a5ea0c\",\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:59ddb50ace59ffebb59a082ed549fe7891900d74\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:59ddb50ace59ffebb59a082ed549fe7891900d74","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}